#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106917} :activity/id #uuid "75a4e4a8-271a-52f6-80d3-a6bbb0833cfd" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050149} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106917 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107063 :tutorial/content "The formula for the area of a general triangle is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}=\\frac{1}{2}{\\color{red}{a}}{\\color{blue}{b}}\\sin{C},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\color{red}a$ and $\\color{blue}b$ are two sides of the triangle and $C$ is the measure of the angle between those sides (called the **included angle**).\n\nWe'll prove this result at the end of the lesson.\n\nTo demonstrate how this works, suppose that we want to calculate the area of the non-right-angled triangle below:\n\n![](images/4b/4b0fb75f9b2eaf3d0237d8cfe2e31bc27b02425b6cffa8d15f27d6de03f60838.png)\n\nLet's call the lengths of the sides $BC = \\color{red}{a}$ and $AC ={\\color{blue}{b}}.$ For the triangle above we have\n${\\color{red}a=10},$ ${\\color{blue}b=12},$ and  $m\\angle C=45^{\\circ}.$\n\nTherefore, the area of our triangle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 10\\cdot 12\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘} \\\\  & =60\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =30\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ff9f9791-f688-59c5-8c9d-b776016045be" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2465 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e26490ba-2418-5a78-9c31-5425d1b67c42" :step/math-academy-id 655395 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106918}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106918 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105903 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105560 :question/id #uuid "62405619-31ca-5403-aa45-360e6b3fbd59" :question/math-academy-id "e-3289" :question/problem "Find the area of $\\triangle{ABC}$ below. Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\n![](images/dc/dc625408598aa3cbfda44ec877c9a26ab563dee9fcf757192fcf2cf8411f606a.png)" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}=\\dfrac{1}{2}ab\\sin{C},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\angle{C}$ is between the sides of lengths $a$ and $b.$ \n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =CB=58 \\\\ b & =CA=40 \\\\ m∠C & ={31}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\sin{31^{\\circ}} = 0.515\\,038\n$$\n\nrounded to six decimal places,  we calculate the area as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 58\\cdot 40\\operatorname{sin}⁡{31}^{∘} \\\\  & =1160\\cdot (0.515\\,038) \\\\  & ≈597.44,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "acc86fb6-2ef4-5f8f-b204-6e1fdbbf0530" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049449}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719607} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106085} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106086} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284783} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295109} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295110} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295123} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528147} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528157} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528162} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528169} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528172} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528187} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528197} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528204} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528213} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528214} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547048} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547053} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719112} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719114} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719118} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719587} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719588} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719591} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719601} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719605}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Area of a Triangle"} :step/id #uuid "ee7e485d-cba2-595c-b5bd-d8cd98cc7e04" :step/math-academy-id 655396 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106919}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106919 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105904 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105579 :question/id #uuid "c8d31ea4-41c6-5f24-bf7a-a950a30342d7" :question/math-academy-id "e-4253" :question/problem "The area of ​​the triangle $\\triangle ABC$ is $24\\sqrt{2} \\text{ cm}^2.$ Find $AB.$\n\n![](images/e9/e93e732bd04cfda304c2098087548a638d8d869ccf8b1050235e8207825875d3.png)" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}=\\dfrac{1}{2}bc\\sin{A},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\angle{A}$ is between the sides of lengths $b$ and $c.$ \n\nHere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB, \\\\ b & =AC=12\\text{ cm}, \\\\ m∠A & ={45}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\sin{45^{\\circ}} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\n$$\n\nwe can calculate $c=AB,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot c\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ 24\\sqrt{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 12\\cdot c\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘} \\\\ 24\\sqrt{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 12\\cdot c\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\ 24\\cancel{\\sqrt{2}} & =3\\cancel{\\sqrt{2}}c \\\\ 24 & =3c \\\\ c & =8\\text{ cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $AB = 8\\,\\textrm{cm}.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cafdb077-0e02-5e7a-bf78-c3a8aacfb6f7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050847}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719854} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106346} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106347} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278381} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278383} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295125} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295126} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323821} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329720} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329721} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556353} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556359} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556366} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556381} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556384} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556391} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556396} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556400} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556401} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186604012} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186604016} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719344} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719349} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719353} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719834} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719839} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719840} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719847} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719853}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Angle or Side of a Triangle Given the Area of a Triangle"} :step/id #uuid "ca664e6a-cebd-5079-927f-7fc062b9c6fd" :step/math-academy-id 655399 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106920}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106920 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105905 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105561 :question/id #uuid "b41b2354-c339-5398-ab43-5886b8447c1c" :question/math-academy-id "e-3294" :question/problem "Rounded to the nearest integer, what is the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD?$\n\n![](images/bc/bc9f32a9c0e8b042498a72e808a29274bd505e786d8ec43d0e0bc9888fdf6e44.png)" :question/worked-solution "The area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is the sum of the areas of the triangles $\\triangle ABC$ and $\\triangle ADC$ (see the illustration below).\n\n![](images/ac/acd2e923a65d0821cd59ed8b8274ceb5fc38def896467e37c8b1d454f958699e.png)\n\n First, we find the area for $\\triangle ABC,$ and get\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABC} & =\\frac{1}{2}(42)(35)\\operatorname{sin}⁡{36}^{∘} \\\\  & =735\\operatorname{sin}⁡{36}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈432.022\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nNow we find the area for $\\triangle ADC,$ and get\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ADC} & =\\frac{1}{2}(20)(18)\\operatorname{sin}⁡{80}^{∘} \\\\  & =180\\operatorname{sin}⁡{80}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈177.265\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nFinally, we add together the areas of our two triangles to determine the area of $ABCD.$ We get\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABCD} & ={A}_{ABC}+{A}_{ADC} \\\\  & =432.022+177.265 \\\\  & ≈609\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, to the nearest integer, the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is $609.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3173d361-b91d-5699-97d8-6595ef59d0a3" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719826} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106087} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106439} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234325} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295111} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297160} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297161} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332914} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335939} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335940} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341859} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547012} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547025} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556057} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556065} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556068} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556073} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556077} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556082} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556083} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556085} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556094} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556097} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557305} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186586574} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719416} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719418} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719421} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719803} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719804} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719810} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719816} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719819}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Area of a Quadrilateral"} :step/id #uuid "ab2a0c05-ebeb-54b0-a9a3-b37d1f143e15" :step/math-academy-id 655402 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106921}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106921 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105906 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105562 :question/id #uuid "472216c8-da7d-58fb-9056-e71d5e33460d" :question/math-academy-id "e-3297" :question/problem "The area of $ABCD$ is $300\\, \\text{cm}^2.$  What is the area of $\\triangle ABC$ rounded to the nearest integer?\n\n![](images/c0/c06ffb123bdd24c3f83419767b6d1578223b8093089b79f91aae8f1fc6121305.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by finding the area of $\\triangle ACD.$ We get \n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ACD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot DA\\cdot DC\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡D \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(25)(32)\\operatorname{sin}⁡{70}^{∘} \\\\  & =400\\operatorname{sin}⁡{70}^{∘} \\\\  & =375.877... \\\\  & ≈376\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nThe difference of the areas of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ and $\\triangle ACD$ gives the area of $\\triangle ABC.$ Therefore, \n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABC} & ={A}_{ACD}-{A}_{ABCD} \\\\  & =376-300 \\\\  & =76\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5dd3dcff-06d2-5d61-9863-0f5b541d8d4b" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720007} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106088} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106089} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297162} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297163} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297164} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297165} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341858} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547072} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547082} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557293} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561383} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561390} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561393} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561404} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561408} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561409} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561416} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561419} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561428} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561437} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186604068} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719513} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719516} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719520} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719976} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719984} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719985} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719991} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720005}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area of a Triangle Given the Area of a Quadrilateral"} :step/id #uuid "85b3474e-aa3e-5a9b-8c47-069d6e314fad" :step/math-academy-id 655405 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106922}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106922 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107027 :tutorial/content "Consider $\\triangle ABC$ with sides of lengths $a,b,$ and $c,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/1d/1d30e12e2252b27a651bb02b718cf2214c7ddcf4f0e768c5bd952c11b2e4ecde.png)\n\nLet's now add the height of length $h$ perpendicular to $\\overline{BC},$ as shown.\n\n![](images/96/96817ab2168b9ec37777166a26001ee8bc7e7709f80fdf234ac6d70cdfbb5ddc.png)\n\nUsing the usual formula for the area $\\mathcal A$ of a triangle in terms of its base and height, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A = \\dfrac12 ah.\n$$\n\nUsing elementary trigonometry, we have that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{h}{b} = \\sin C\n$$\n\nwhich means that \n\n$$\nh = b\\sin C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by substituting this into our expression for $\\mathcal A,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A = \\dfrac12ab\\sin C\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nWe can use a similar approach to prove the following formulas:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ A & =\\frac{1}{2}ac\\operatorname{sin}⁡B\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e126d83f-0c01-514b-bb7e-ce2d16291b22" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13277 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Formula for the Area of a General Triangle"} :step/id #uuid "29c5a503-b01d-5845-a29d-0bd76807b33f" :step/math-academy-id 655408}] :activity/title "The Area of a General Triangle" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105954}]}
